{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-161158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+3y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{14}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x+3y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,3,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+3y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,2z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨x,\\,y,\\,4x+6y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(-2x-3y+4x+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\cdot \\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{14}}}(2x+3y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{14}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+3y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+3xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+3y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ee07224bfe5e54c4d21eca5a0b1d41908a0a0b3b1c0ef8adf81ffea82278a1b"} {"question" "q-161158" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161158" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{14}}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{14}}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{14}}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161158" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=3$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=3$ for  $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,6⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}6\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ee07224bfe5e54c4d21eca5a0b1d41908a0a0b3b1c0ef8adf81ffea82278a1b"} {"question" "q-161187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,z-1⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x-2y+1$ for $-1\\le x\\le 0$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=3.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,-2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,z-1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,2x-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}+1+{x}^{2}+1+2x-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[({y}^{2}-2y+1)+({x}^{2}+2x+1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{3}}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[(x+1)^{2}\\cdot y+\\frac{(y-1)^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}+\\frac{x}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ee07224bfe5e54c4d21eca5a0b1d41908a0a0b3b1c0ef8adf81ffea82278a1b"} {"question" "q-161195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ee07224bfe5e54c4d21eca5a0b1d41908a0a0b3b1c0ef8adf81ffea82278a1b"} {"question" "q-161196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-161196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-162551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x-y+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-162551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨3x,\\,4x-2y+2,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(6x-(4x-2y+2)-(y-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y+16)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[2{y}^{2}+16y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-162551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ee07224bfe5e54c4d21eca5a0b1d41908a0a0b3b1c0ef8adf81ffea82278a1b"} {"question" "q-162551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-162551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-16"] ["math" "-18"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "18"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"} {"question" "q-162551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "18"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070439/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63bf854c417cdf0a2fec9ac80edccaea7d46a8a6f4798a6df9835c9e4a04cef"}] "usage" {"q-161158" []} "basis" 2481 "retractions" [[17592186167067 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨x, y, 2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z = 2x + 3y$ for $0 \\le x \\le 1$ and $0 \\le y \\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n$.\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $\\mid \\mid r_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′} \\mid |= \\sqrt{14}$.*"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163821 17592186163822 17592186163823 17592186163824 17592186163825] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163820 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163820 :answer-field/id] [17592186163820 :answer-field/key] [17592186163820 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
